<p>At some point after I got <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/08/07/my-new-montior-is-pushing.html">my new screen</a>, I gave up on the native Mail app on my Mac. With the new resolution the text became too small to read and I got fed up that something as basic as zoom wasn&rsquo;t available on one of Mac&rsquo;s classic apps<sup id="fnref:1"><a href="#fn:1" class="footnote-ref" role="doc-noteref">1</a></sup>. Even 40-something-year-old Emacs has a built-in zoom function.</p>
<p>Thunderbird on macOS sounds out of place, especially with so many other options. I picked it up because it&rsquo;s one of the clear options on Linux (my desktop), and I know it to be a free and well-maintained app<sup id="fnref:2"><a href="#fn:2" class="footnote-ref" role="doc-noteref">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p>I didn&rsquo;t expect that I&rsquo;d like it, and it still has its quirks — but you know what? It kind of works. It does a clean job syncing my personal calendar too, which I appreciate. I don&rsquo;t know that I will always use it, but for now my eyes are thanking me. At the same time, I&rsquo;ve switched from Vivaldi Mail (it does what it&rsquo;s supposed to, but it&rsquo;s too minimal, and I never got used to the UI) to Thunderbird on my Linux as well.</p>
<p>I don&rsquo;t know that I&rsquo;ll stick with it. The tabs are weird and the fact that Grammarly <em>doesn&rsquo;t</em> work with it was a nasty surprise (it is one of the points why Emacs alone doesn&rsquo;t work for emails). For now… it does the job.</p>
